<h2>Kazakh</h2>

<p>
Kazakh is a Turkic language written, in Kazakhstan, in a Cyrillic alphabet of
42 letters: the 33 Russian letters plus ә, ғ, қ, ң, ө, ұ, ү, һ and і.  A
Latin alphabet is being introduced, but the bulk of existing text, and of
text indexed by search engines today, is Cyrillic, and this algorithm handles
Cyrillic only.
</p>

<p>
Like Turkish, Kazakh is agglutinative: a noun takes, in order, derivational
suffixes, the plural, a possessive, a case ending and a predicative person
ending, and a verb takes voice, negation, tense and person.  Each suffix has
two to six allomorphs, chosen by vowel harmony (back а о ұ ы against front
ә е ө ү і) and by the last sound of what precedes it: the locative is
<i>-да</i> after a vowel or voiced consonant, <i>-та</i> after a voiceless
one; the plural is <i>-лар</i> after a vowel, р, й or у, <i>-дар</i> after
л м н ң з ж and <i>-тар</i> after a voiceless consonant.  A final п, қ or к
of the base becomes б, ғ or г before a vowel-initial suffix
(<i>кітап</i>, <i>кітабы</i>), and a narrow vowel in the last syllable of
some roots drops out before one (<i>орын</i>, <i>орны</i>).
</p>

<h2>The stemming algorithm</h2>

<p>
The algorithm strips suffixes from the right one at a time.  Each slot is
an <code>among</code> table; an entry is accepted only if
</p>

<ul>
<li> its vowel class matches the last non-glide vowel of what precedes it
     (у, и and ю are transparent; a base with no other vowel, such as
     <i>туризм</i>, accepts either class, and a base ending in к or г
     accepts front suffixes whatever its vowels, which is how loanwords
     such as <i>банк</i> behave),
<li> its initial consonant fits the final sound of the base as described
     above, and
<li> the base keeps at least one vowel and either three letters or a vowel
     followed by a consonant (<i>ел</i>, <i>іс</i>).
</ul>

<p>
The word is first tried as a finite verb: a tense ending, then negation,
then the denominal verbaliser <i>-ла</i>.  If no tense ending matches, the
nominal suffixes are removed in a loop until none applies, and then a
participle ending that the nominal suffixes may have covered.  Finally, if a
vowel-initial suffix was removed, б ғ г at the end of the stem are restored
to п қ к and an elided ы/і is put back before a final н, з or қ.
</p>

<p>
The loop over nominal suffixes matters more than it looks.  Many Kazakh
lemmas end in a string that is also a suffix (<i>теңге</i> ends in the
dative <i>-ге</i>, <i>сауда</i> in the locative <i>-да</i>), so a single
pass would cut the bare lemma once more than its inflected forms and the
two would never meet in an index.  With the loop <i>сауда</i>,
<i>саудада</i> and <i>саудасы</i> all give <i>сау</i>, and <i>теңге</i>,
<i>теңгеге</i> and <i>теңгемен</i> all give <i>тең</i>: wrong as a
dictionary form, but the same wrong form for every occurrence, which is
what matters for retrieval.  The loop does not reach every form.  The
accusative <i>-ны</i> is refused after <i>-ге</i> (see below), so
<i>теңгені</i> gives <i>теңген</i>.
</p>

<h3>Why some suffixes are not removed</h3>

<p>
The algorithm was developed against BM25 retrieval over Kazakh news text
(2,999 articles, 132 judged queries), with Kazakh Wikipedia passages
(8,370 passages, 300 queries) as a second check, comparing recall with and
without stemming and against a dictionary-backed morphological analyser.
Several suffixes that a morphological analyser would strip are left alone.
The evidence behind each is of a different kind, and the list below says
which.  Only three rules were tested as variants on the news set: stripping
the verbal noun <i>-у</i>, stripping voice, and reading <i>-ны</i> after a
vowel as the accusative.  Each variant landed within the noise of 132
queries, and those runs are not part of the published results, so they
establish neither a direction nor a size.  The remaining rules were decided
from probe words and from the author's reading of news text, with no
frequency count behind them.
</p>

<ul>
<li> The verbal noun <i>-у</i> (<i>дамыту</i> "development", <i>кездесу</i>
     "meeting", <i>сайлау</i> "election").  These are lexicalised nouns and
     are what a searcher types; conflating them with the finite verb merges
     a rare, specific term into a very frequent one and the query loses its
     weight.  The nominal suffixes on a verbal noun are still removed, so
     <i>дамытуға</i> and <i>дамытудың</i> give <i>дамыту</i>.
<li> Voice suffixes (passive <i>-ыл/-іл</i>, <i>-л/-н</i>; causative
     <i>-тыр/-дыр</i>, <i>-қыз/-гіз</i>), on the same term-weight argument.
     A second reason is semantic.  The passive reports an event and the
     active an agent, so <i>жарияланды</i> "was published" is a better index
     term as <i>жариялан</i> than as <i>жарияла</i>.
<li> First and second person endings (<i>-мын, -сың, -сыз, -м, -ң</i>).
     The author judged them rare in written text, and the same strings end
     many content nouns: <i>білім</i> "education", <i>бағдарлама</i>
     "programme", <i>тәуелсіз</i> "independent".  The privative
     <i>-сыз/-сіз</i> is left for the same reason.  No count was made, and
     the cost is that <i>оқисыз</i> "you read" stays whole.
<li> The habitual participle <i>-атын/-етін</i> after a consonant, which
     cannot be told from the very common nouns in <i>-ат/-ет</i> with
     possessive and accusative (<i>мемлекет-і-н</i> "its state",
     <i>қызмет-і-н</i>).  The author judged the nouns the larger class, so
     the nominal chain reads the participle that way, and <i>болатын</i>,
     <i>болатынын</i> and <i>болатындар</i> all give <i>болат</i>, while
     <i>айтатын</i> gives <i>айтат</i>.  After a vowel the <i>-й-</i> makes
     the participle unambiguous and the finite tense table strips bare
     <i>-йтын/-йтін</i> (<i>жасайтын</i> gives <i>жаса</i>).  With a nominal
     suffix on top the nominal chain again reads <i>-ын</i> as possessive
     plus accusative, so <i>жасайтынын</i> gives <i>жасайт</i>.  The
     participle entries for <i>-атын/-етін</i> in the code are therefore
     never reached, and removing them changes no word in the sample
     vocabulary.
<li> The reflexive <i>-ын/-ін</i>, on the argument that it ends too many
     roots (<i>дайын</i>, <i>ақын</i>, <i>тұрғын</i>, <i>жақын</i>).  That
     argument protects those words only from the verb chain.  The nominal
     chain reads their <i>-ын</i> as possessive plus accusative, so the
     output has <i>дайын</i> as <i>дай</i>, <i>ақын</i> as <i>ақ</i>,
     <i>тұрғын</i> as <i>тұрық</i>, <i>жақын</i> as <i>жақ</i>, and also
     <i>мүмкін</i> "possible" as <i>мүмк</i>.
<li> The past participle <i>-ған/-ген</i> and converb <i>-ып/-іп</i> on a
     one-syllable base (<i>болған</i>, <i>айтқан</i>, <i>келген</i>,
     <i>болып</i>).  The author's judgment, not a count, is that
     one-syllable verbs are mostly auxiliaries whose participles may as well
     stay distinct terms, while nouns such as <i>дүкен</i> "shop",
     <i>науқан</i> "campaign", <i>кәсіп</i> "trade" would otherwise be cut.
     The nominal suffixes still come off, so <i>болғаны</i> gives
     <i>болған</i> and <i>келгенін</i> gives <i>келген</i>.
<li> The similative <i>-дай/-дей/-тай/-тей</i>.  The author judged it rare
     in news text, and the same strings end roots such as <i>құрылтай</i>
     "assembly", which therefore stays whole.
</ul>

<h3>Known ambiguities</h3>

<p>
Some decisions cannot be made from the word alone, and the algorithm takes
the reading that is more frequent in running text:
</p>

<ul>
<li> <i>-ны</i> after a vowel is read as the accusative (<i>бала-ны</i>),
     so a noun ending in н with the possessive loses its н:
     <i>ауданы</i> "its district" gives <i>ауда</i> while every other form
     of <i>аудан</i> keeps it.  Vowel-final nouns are the larger class.
<li> <i>-сы</i> after consonant + ы/і is read as noun in <i>-ыс</i> plus
     possessive (<i>жұмыс-ы</i>, <i>облыс-ы</i>, <i>Мәжіліс-і</i>), so a
     lemma such as <i>желі</i> "network" gives <i>желіс</i> in its
     possessive forms.  Agent nouns in <i>-шы</i> are exempted
     (<i>тілші-сі</i>).
<li> <i>-ады/-еді</i> on a one-syllable base is undecidable against vowel
     stem plus past <i>-ды</i> (<i>бар-ады</i> against <i>жаса-ды</i>); the
     past reading is taken, so <i>барады</i> gives <i>бара</i>.
<li> Accusative <i>-ны</i> is refused after <i>-ға/-ге/-қа/-ке</i> so that
     participles with the possessive are not cut (<i>болғаны</i> stays
     <i>болған</i>); the price is <i>теңгені</i> giving <i>теңген</i>.
<li> <i>-ғы/-гі/-қы/-кі</i> after a consonant is read as the adjective of
     time or place (<i>жылғы</i>, <i>сыртқы</i>), so a root that elides its
     vowel before the possessive is cut.  <i>халқы</i> "its people" gives
     <i>хал</i>, while <i>халқының</i> goes through the case and possessive
     slots and gives <i>халық</i>.
<li> A final <i>-ы/-і</i> after a consonant is read as the possessive, so
     <i>Алматы</i> gives <i>алмат</i> and <i>президенті</i> gives
     <i>президент</i>.  Only the verb chain's <i>-ты/-ті</i> is restricted
     to a voiceless consonant before it.
</ul>

<p>
Surnames in <i>-ев</i> after a back vowel take back suffixes in practice
(<i>Тоқаевтың</i>, <i>Назарбаевқа</i>) although their last vowel is front,
and the harmony check allows for this.
</p>

<h3>Measured effect</h3>

<p>
On the news collection (132 queries), BM25 recall@10 is 0.524 without
stemming, 0.626 with this algorithm, 0.637 with the analyser's rule engine
run without its dictionary, and 0.663 with the dictionary-backed analyser.
The gain over no stemming has a paired-bootstrap 95% interval of [+0.048,
+0.156].  The difference to the analyser is -0.037 with interval [-0.077,
-0.002], and to the engine without dictionary -0.011 with interval
[-0.048, +0.024].  On the Wikipedia collection (300 queries) recall@10 is
0.830, 0.920, 0.910 and 0.910 in the same order.
</p>

<p>
On KazQAD, an independent benchmark of 1,929 test questions judged against
825,309 Kazakh Wikipedia passages (BM25 k1=0.9, b=0.4), nDCG@10 is 0.219
without stemming and 0.290 with this algorithm, a gain of +0.071 with 95%
interval [+0.059, +0.083].  The dictionary-backed analyser scores 0.288,
a difference from this algorithm of -0.002 with interval [-0.009, +0.005],
and the engine without dictionary scores 0.273, a difference of -0.017
with interval [-0.026, -0.009].
</p>

<p>
So the dictionary-backed analyser is ahead by about 0.04 recall@10 on the
news set, with an interval that excludes zero.  On the Wikipedia
collection this algorithm is 0.010 ahead of it, with no interval computed
for that pair, and on KazQAD the difference is inside its interval.  The
news-set gap has no established cause.
</p>

<h3>Questions for reviewers</h3>

<p>
The following decisions were taken on the author's judgment and would
benefit from a native speaker's or a maintainer's view:
</p>

<ul>
<li> Consonant-stem habitual participles stem to the <i>-ат/-ет</i> noun
     class (<i>айтатын</i> gives <i>айтат</i>, <i>болатын</i> gives
     <i>болат</i>), while vowel-stem <i>жасайтын</i> gives <i>жаса</i>.  Is
     the judgment that <i>-ат/-ет</i> noun + possessive + accusative
     outnumbers participle + <i>-ын</i> plausible for news and Wikipedia
     text?  The participle entries for <i>-атын/-етін</i> are unreachable
     and could be removed.
<li> One-syllable <i>-ған/-ып</i> forms (<i>болған</i>, <i>болып</i>,
     <i>айтқан</i>, <i>келген</i>) stay whole and are indexed apart from
     <i>бол</i>, <i>айт</i>, <i>кел</i>.  Is that acceptable, and are
     <i>дүкен</i>, <i>мекен</i>, <i>науқан</i>, <i>кәсіп</i>, <i>тәртіп</i>
     the nouns worth protecting?
<li> Verbal nouns in <i>-у</i> are treated as lexicalised across the board.
     Is that defensible, or only for a subset such as <i>сайлау</i>,
     <i>кездесу</i>, <i>дамыту</i>?
<li> First and second person endings and <i>-сыз/-сіз</i> are assumed rare
     in written Kazakh without a count.  How frequent are they in the text
     this stemmer will index?
<li> Roots in <i>-ын/-ін</i> are cut by the possessive reading
     (<i>тұрғын</i> gives <i>тұрық</i>, <i>мүмкін</i> gives <i>мүмк</i>).
     Is that acceptable collateral, or does it warrant a guard?
</ul>

